  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Prediction and comparison design",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "response",
          "prompt": "Required before sampling: Which population size do you expect to show larger chance changes? Could a favored allele ever decrease in a small population? Explain your predictions.",
          "text": "Smaller populations may show more chance variation; advantage is not a guarantee."
        },
        {
          "type": "response",
          "prompt": "Required before sampling: We compare 5 individuals (10 gene copies) with 50 individuals (100 copies). Both begin at 50% A. Why use several independent populations at each size?",
          "text": "Independent populations show variation at the same starting frequency."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Timing: Before any random draws."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Neutral populations give A and a equal reproductive contribution. Both sizes begin at 50% A and run for five generations with no migration or mutation. The selection comparison starts again at 50% A and changes only the contribution rule."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Sampling evidence",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Beads symbolize copies in possible gametes; source copies are not removed. Each draw is independent within a generation. Counts are model outcomes; hand-guided and automatic draws are distinguished."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
